in @prism
in @meta
in @glass
in @magic

# @magic/contract — the binding species. Names the actions that
# create, honor, and verify the boundary contract between surface
# and mechanism.
#
# First species shard of the @magic family (recognition #80,
# substrate-decl landed at ticks 7-8 evening 2026-06-18). The family-
# root declared three carriers (magic_surface, magic_mechanism,
# magic_contract); this species adds the actions that operate on the
# contract carrier.
#
# Why contract first: reveal and audit both reference the contract.
# Without contract's bind/honor/verify actions, reveal's controlled-
# disclosure has no operational target and audit's verification has
# no surface invariant to check. Contract is the foundational species;
# surface/mechanism are the dual carriers it binds; reveal/audit are
# the downstream operations that consume contracts.
#
# === The substrate-pull frame ===
#
# Per recognition #57 ([[architecture-alignment-as-boundary-
# mathematics]]), alignment IS the boundary contract enforced at @io.
# Under recognition #80, that boundary IS @magic/contract; alignment
# IS honor(contract); misalignment IS contract-violation-with-surface-
# intact (the con/scam structure per #78's Narcissus pole).
#
# This species is therefore the substrate-decl of the alignment
# verification primitive: bind binds the gauge-visible surface to
# the matter-hidden mechanism with an explicit promise; honor reads
# whether the binding is currently respected; the verdict surfaces
# at the @io boundary harness for the agent's substrate-political
# accountability.
#
# === Spencer-Brown analogy (not inheritance) ===
#
# Per Seam adversarial review C3: the previous tick claimed
# inheritance from @epistemologic/cybernetic/distinction via the `in`
# clause AND identified bind with the cross operation — but cross
# takes (mark, distinction_space) and bind takes (magic_surface,
# magic_mechanism, magic_invariant). Different types. The inheritance
# was decorative; the identity-claim was analogy, not structural.
#
# Hedge: the contract is ANALOGOUS to Spencer-Brown's mark at @magic
# altitude; the binding is ANALOGOUS to the cross at this altitude.
# A future @magic/contract ↔ @epistemologic/cybernetic/distinction
# adapter species can re-establish the structural inheritance via
# `requires distinction_well_formed(...)` clauses when the adapter
# lands. Until then, no inheritance via `in` (Seam C3).
#
# === Pre-AI prior art ===
#
#   Hoare contract              Hoare 1969 ("An Axiomatic Basis for
#                               Computer Programming"). Pre/post-
#                               condition contracts. The procedural
#                               ancestor of @magic/contract's
#                               binding semantics.
#   Design by Contract          Meyer 1986 (Eiffel; Design by
#                               Contract). The OO altitude; the
#                               substrate's bind/honor pair IS
#                               structurally equivalent to Eiffel's
#                               require/ensure clauses.
#   stage-magic contract        Maskelyne, Houdini (19th-20th c.).
#                               The cultural-practice ancestor; the
#                               magician's promise to the audience
#                               about what the trick will deliver.
#
# Spec context: docs/math/the-tower/recognition-80-magic-as-form-
#               process-substrate-decl.md (Reed's scratch).
# Substrate decisions: [[architecture-shards-as-substrate-source]],
#                      [[architecture-prism-as-trait-as-everything]],
#                      [[architecture-alignment-as-boundary-
#                       mathematics]] (#57; the contract is what
#                       alignment enforces),
#                      [[architecture-glass-wall-substrate-types]]
#                       (transparency<contract> is the verdict
#                       carrier at the species boundary),
#                      [[feedback-no-bare-types]] (all carriers
#                       typed; the verdict is transparency<contract>,
#                       not bare bool).
# Related shards: magic.mirror (the family-root; declares the
#                  carriers this species operates on),
#                 epistemologic/cybernetic/distinction.mirror (the
#                  Spencer-Brown ancestor inherited via `in`).
# Path-namespace property: this file at
# `shards/magic/contract.mirror` declares `@magic/contract` per
# `@epistemologic/pact/path_matches_namespace`.

source @arxiv/programming/hoare-1969
source @arxiv/programming/meyer-1986

# Note: `magic_invariant` moved to family-root (magic.mirror) per Seam
# C5 so it can type magic_contract.promise. Imported via `in @magic`.

# === bind action ===
#
# The substrate-vocabulary action that creates a magic_contract by
# binding a surface to a mechanism under a typed invariant. The
# invariant IS the substrate-typed assertion that the mechanism
# delivers what the surface advertises.
#
# Analogous to (not structurally inheriting) Spencer-Brown's cross
# at @magic altitude per the hedged ancestry note above (Seam C3).
#
# Body discharges at the realisation boundary; per-species bodies
# enforce the bilateral commitment per recognition #37 (Pask
# agreement).
bind(s: magic_surface, m: magic_mechanism, promise: magic_invariant) -> magic_contract { \ }

# === honor action ===
#
# The substrate-vocabulary action that reads whether a contract is
# currently honored: surface invariants intact, mechanism delivering
# the promise, no violations recorded. The verdict surfaces at the
# @io boundary harness.
#
# Verdict carrier: transparency<magic_contract> per
# [[architecture-glass-wall-substrate-types]]:
#
# - success                     all invariants verified; the contract
#                               is honored at the witness instant.
# - partial(opacity_map)        some invariants verified; the opacity
#                               map names which remain dark.
# - failure(opacity_map)        a violation is recorded; the opacity
#                               map names which invariant failed and
#                               carries the witness.
#
# Body discharges at the realisation boundary.
honor(c: magic_contract) -> transparency(magic_contract) { \ }

# === verify action ===
#
# Stricter than honor: explicit verification against a named
# invariant. honor checks all invariants the contract carries;
# verify checks one specific invariant. Used by audit (@magic/audit
# species) and by downstream consumers that name specific properties.
#
# Returns the substrate's existing verdict carrier (success / partial
# / failure trichotomy).
verify(c: magic_contract, inv: magic_invariant) -> verdict { \ }

# === invariant_preserved predicate ===
#
# Bilateral verdict predicate consumer shards name in a `requires`
# clause (per recognition #37) to declare bilateral agreement that
# their contract preserves a specific invariant. Same shape as
# `eigenform_witnessing(carrier)` and `distinction_well_formed(m, s)`.
#
# The bilateral commitment: the contract commits to declaring the
# invariant; the consumer commits to discharging the verify at the
# species boundary.
invariant_preserved(c: magic_contract, inv: magic_invariant) -> verdict { \ }

# === mechanism_bound_to predicate ===
#
# Tick 21 (2026-06-19) per Seam retrospective gap #2: bilateral
# verdict predicate asserting that the contract's mechanism field
# equals the named magic_mechanism. The substrate-pull-correct guard
# against audit-bypass: an audited contract for an UNRELATED
# mechanism cannot unseal that mechanism without this clause.
#
# Discharge: byte-equality on c.mechanism and m under the substrate's
# content-addressing. Body discharges at the realisation boundary.
#
# Used by @magic/mechanism's unseal action via
# `requires mechanism_bound_to(c, m)`.
mechanism_bound_to(c: magic_contract, m: magic_mechanism) -> verdict { \ }

out @magic/contract
out bind
out honor
out verify
out invariant_preserved
out mechanism_bound_to
